// Concurrency with scarlet/process: lightweight processes.
//
// A process is not an OS thread. It costs a few hundred bytes and about a
// microsecond to start, so a process per task — or per connection — is the
// cheap option, not the extravagant one. Where a process runs is not the
// program's concern: the runtime schedules them across every core,
// preemptively, on a reduction budget, and moves them between cores to keep
// the load level. A process that loops for ever cannot starve the others, and
// one that blocks on I/O parks alone. Processes share no mutable state; values
// are immutable, so anything that crosses between them behaves as a copy.
//
// The program exits once every process has finished. There is no join — nothing
// here waits — so these lines are printed by processes racing each other and
// their *order* is not fixed, which is why this example has no golden output.

import scarlet/process
import scarlet/time

// A spawned process has nowhere to send a result, so the closure spawn takes
// returns Nil: a process that produces something must do something with it
// itself — print it, write it to a socket, hand it to a service.
fn worker(id Int, work_ms Int) Nil {
	// sleep parks only this process. The other three keep running, and the main
	// process below has already finished.
	process.sleep(work_ms)
	println('worker ${id} finished after ${work_ms}ms')
}

// The four workers report in sleep order, not spawn order.
println('spawning four workers')

process.spawn(fn() worker(1, 40))
process.spawn(fn() worker(2, 10))
process.spawn(fn() worker(3, 30))
process.spawn(fn() worker(4, 20))

// spawn returns the child's Pid: its identity, distinct from every other
// process's for the life of the program. A process learns its own from
// process.self(). A Pid is a name, not a mailbox — sending goes through
// Subjects (see tests/programs/messages.scrl) — so what it is for is telling
// processes apart: keying a map of workers, naming which one an event was
// about, monitoring it, or killing it.
//
// Every process spawned here is linked to this one: if one of them crashed
// (say, an out-of-range slice), the crash would end it, be reported, and end
// this process too — the program would exit with a failure instead of
// running on with a piece missing. A process whose failure should be handled
// rather than fatal is started with process.spawn_unlinked and watched with
// process.monitor; tests/programs/monitors.scrl and exits.scrl show both.
child = process.spawn(fn() println('child sees itself as ${process.self()}'))
println('parent spawned ${child}; the parent itself is ${process.self()}')
println('a pid equals only itself: ${child == child} ${child == process.self()}')

// time.monotonic is a clock that only moves forward, so a duration measured
// across a sleep survives a wall-clock adjustment mid-nap.
fn timed(label String) Nil {
	start = time.monotonic()
	process.sleep(5)
	elapsed = time.since_ms(time.monotonic(), start)
	println('${label}: slept at least 5ms: ${elapsed >= 5}')
}

process.spawn(fn() timed('sleeper'))

println('main process done — the program stays up until the others finish')
